WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.252
Encoder-decoder models separate understanding the input from generating the output.

00:00:05.252 --> 00:00:10.887
The encoder reads the source sequence and produces contextual representations for it.

00:00:10.887 --> 00:00:15.532
The decoder generates the target sequence one token at a time.

00:00:15.532 --> 00:00:20.342
In addition to causal self-attention over the tokens it has generated so far,

00:00:20.342 --> 00:00:24.167
the decoder can use cross-attention to the encoder output.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:29.747
That cross-attention lets each output step focus on relevant parts of the source.

00:00:29.747 --> 00:00:38.387
This structure is natural for sequence-to-sequence tasks where the input and output are both text but are not

00:00:38.387 --> 00:00:41.162
the same sequence.

00:00:41.162 --> 00:00:45.582
Translation is the classic example: encode a sentence in one language, then generate the translation.

00:00:45.582 --> 00:00:51.947
Summarization is another: encode a longer document, then generate a shorter summary.

00:00:51.947 --> 00:00:56.800
T5 and BART are well-known examples of encoder-decoder families.
